### Rationale
The die-locality idea (H16) is refuted, so this candidate is r1's kernel, unchanged apart from comments. It passed run_tests 16/16 and timed the same as r1 (S +0.2%, M 0.0%, L 0.0%), so it's a control and isn't worth a portal slot.

- **Dispatch:** small grids already spread over every SM. At 128 rows, the 640 CTAs land 4–5 on each of the 148 SMs.
- **Latency:** there is a real split. Remote DRAM reads take about 70% longer, and the home die changes every 4 KB.
- **Not computable:** the home depends on the address bits plus one physical-address bit per 2 MB page. A kernel can't know that bit.
- **No gain even with a perfect host-measured map:** at 256 MB, all-local reads equal a plain stream. At 5 MB, all-local and all-remote cost the same. Assigning work by SM adds about 1 µs or more.

The locality variant was therefore not built.

